Ryven wrote:
> This based on the following assumptions:
> 
> 1. Vegetarianism is the conduct of refraining from eating animal
> products that force the animal to be killed in the process (meat).
> 
> 2. Veganism is the conduct of refraining from eating any animal
> products (including eggs, milk, cheese, etc.)
> 
> My vegan Monk is wandering happily through the dungeon, dishing out
> extra helpings of death. I come upon a gray ooze. "Aha!" I think: oozes
> aren't animals, i can eat it! I dispatch it and eat the corpse. When it
> gives me a message telling me I've become healthier, I become
> disturbed: usually I only gain intrinsics when eating meat. I check my
> conduct, fearing I have lost veganism: I find that for some reason am
> now a vegetarian.
> 
> If the ooze is an animal, it should break veganism AND vegetarianism.
> 
> If the ooze isn't an animal, it shouldn't break veganism.
> 
> The ooze is certainly NOT a dispensable animal product: it obviously
> had a life of its own.
> 
> I believe that it should either break both conducts or neither. It
> doesn't make sense to break only one.

I'm not sure what the logic is, but it's an intentional feature. From 
the guidebook:

--------------------------------------------------
      A strict vegan diet is one which  avoids  any  food  derived
from animals.  The primary source of nutrition is fruits and veg-
etables.  The corpses and tins of blobs (`b'), jellies (`j'), and
fungi  (`F') are also considered to be vegetable matter.  Certain
human food is prepared without animal  products;  namely,  lembas
wafers,  cram  rations, food rations (gunyoki), K-rations, and C-
rations.  Metal or another normally indigestible  material  eaten
while polymorphed into a creature that can digest it is also con-
sidered vegan food.  Note however that eating  such  items  still
counts against foodless conduct.

      Vegetarians  do  not eat animals; however, they are less se-
lective about eating animal byproducts than vegans.  In  addition
to the vegan items listed above, they may eat any kind of pudding
(`P') other than the black puddings, eggs and food made from eggs
(fortune  cookies  and pancakes), food made with milk (cream pies
and candy bars), and lumps of royal jelly.  Monks are expected to
observe a vegetarian diet.
--------------------------------------------------

Perhaps it comes from a literal interpretation of "pudding". But I see 
your point, it doesn't make much sense.

